# Citation metadata (Citation File Format 1.2.0, https://citation-file-format.github.io).
# GitHub reads this file and renders a "Cite this repository" button with APA
# and BibTeX ready to copy.
#
# EDTF's users are libraries, archives, museums and digital-humanities
# projects — people for whom "which version did you run?" is a question a
# paper has to answer. That is what this file is for.
#
# `version` and `date-released` are kept current by
# .github/scripts/prepare-release.sh, which runs on the release branch:
# release-plz cannot see this file, so left to itself it would silently
# describe whatever version was current when it was written.
cff-version: 1.2.0
title: edtf
message: >-
  If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata in this file.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Allen
    given-names: Carl
    alias: CarlAllenn
repository-code: https://github.com/CarlAllenn/edtf
abstract: >-
  A spec-exact Rust implementation of EDTF, the Extended Date/Time Format
  defined as the profile in ISO 8601-2:2019 Annex A, covering conformance
  levels 0, 1 and 2 in full. One no_std, zero-dependency core provides
  parsing, validation, level classification, calendar bounds, three-valued
  temporal relations under uncertainty, lazy value enumeration and canonical
  formatting; that same core is compiled into a WebAssembly bundle, a
  Postgres extension, a command-line tool, a prose-to-EDTF normaliser
  (English and Russian pattern tables) and a Julian-to-Gregorian converter,
  so a date that is valid in an application is valid in its database. Every
  parsing judgement call is recorded as a numbered decision against the ISO
  section it derives from.
keywords:
  - edtf
  - iso-8601
  - date
  - temporal
  - parser
  - rust
  - digital-humanities
  - cultural-heritage
license:
  - MIT
  - Apache-2.0
version: 1.2.3
date-released: '2026-08-08'
